     INTRODUCTION

          The btikpci software is licensed under the terms of
          the GNU General Public License (GPL).  This allows
          users of Linux to freely change and adapt the
          btikpci software for use with their particular
          version of the Linux kernel.  The text of the GPL
          is contained in the LICENSE file.



     BUILD THE KERNEL DRIVERS

          To build the kernel module simply type:

               make


          Some kernels earlier than 6.4.0 have backported a breaking change 
          to the kernel class_create function. On such kernels, to build the
          kernel module type:

               make CLASS_CREATE_WITHOUT_OWNER=1



     SIGN THE KERNEL DRIVER

          Secure boot is now enabled in modern Linux distributions
          by default. This means kernel modules such as this one
          cannot be loaded by the kernel unless they are cryptographically
          signed.
          
          One option is to disable Secure Boot in the BIOS. While this
          is a reliable way to allow installation of unsigned kernel modules
          it is not recommended for all environments for security reasons.
          
               1. Enter BIOS setup
               2. Disable secure boot
               3. Save configuration
               4. Reboot the system

          Another option is to rebuild the kernel such that CONFIG_MODULE_SIG_FORCE
          is off. Rebuilding the kernel is beyond the scope of the README, but
          more information on this configuration option is available here
          https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/v4.15/admin-guide/module-signing.html

          Other solutions are distribution-specific, and involve adding a new
          certificate to the shim and signing the module with the private key using
          OS tools such as mokutil. Here are some articles for a few common distributions
          
               Ubuntu https://ubuntu.com/blog/how-to-sign-things-for-secure-boot
               Gentoo https://wiki.gentoo.org/wiki/Signed_kernel_module_support
               Fedora https://docs.fedoraproject.org/en-US/Fedora/22/html/System_Administrators_Guide/sect-signing-kernel-modules-for-secure-boot.html

          Once on of these options has been implemented, the module should be
          loadable on the target system.



     INSTALL THE DRIVER

          After the build is complete, the resulting kernel
          module files will be copied to output folders (e.g.
          pci-rel for the PCI release build).  To install the
          module, run the following from a root account:

               make install



     CONFIGURE UDEV

          Copy the file 70-btipci.rules to /etc/udev/rules.d
          to configure udev to make the device nodes read/write.



